  - Complexity: large.
  - Depends on: RUN-002 for durable storage.

## P2 - Pluggable agent memory

`AgentIR.memory` (`agent_spec/models.py`/`loader.py`) is parsed and validated
today but never consumed — no compiler or invoker reads it, and there is no
storage abstraction for it at all. This is a different concern from
`CheckpointStore`: checkpoints resume a *workflow run*; memory is an *agent's*
conversational/context history across turns or sessions. Google ADK's
`ADK_MEMORY_SERVICE_URI` only configures ADK's own native dev-ui memory
service, not the declarative `memory` field, and LangGraph has no equivalent
at all. The tasks below add a `MemoryStore` abstraction that mirrors the
`CheckpointStore` design **RUN-002** already established — protocol-based,
async, `ping()`-checked availability, backend selected by configuration — and
make it genuinely pluggable so a deployer can supply a backend this repo does
not ship (Redis, a vector store, a managed memory service) without forking
`open-agent-core`.

- [ ] **MEM-001:** Define a pluggable `MemoryStore` protocol and an in-memory
  default implementation.
  - Acceptance:
    - `open_agent_core/memory.py` defines an async `MemoryStore` protocol:
      `append(scope_id, entry)`, `history(scope_id, limit=...)`,
      `clear(scope_id)`, `ping()`.
    - `InMemoryMemoryStore` is the default (per-process, used when no memory
      backend is configured) and is explicitly documented as ephemeral — not
      shared across processes and not surviving a restart.
    - The scope key mirrors the existing checkpoint `run_id`/thread-id
      concept so per-conversation memory and per-run checkpoints can use the
      same identifier without coupling the two stores together.
  - Files: new `open_agent_core/memory.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: medium.

- [ ] **MEM-002:** Add a durable `SQLiteMemoryStore` and a
  `PostgresMemoryStore`, and a backend-selection factory.
  - Acceptance:
    - `SQLiteMemoryStore` follows the async, thread-pool-offloaded pattern
      already used by `SQLiteCheckpointStore` (**RUN-002**).
    - `PostgresMemoryStore` reuses the same `asyncpg` connection approach as
      `PostgresCheckpointStore`, in its own table/schema, separate from
      checkpoints.
    - A factory selects the backend from `MEMORY_STORE_PATH` or
      `MEMORY_DATABASE_URL`, defaulting to `InMemoryMemoryStore` when unset,
      independent of the checkpoint backend's own selection.
    - Both new stores implement `ping()` for availability checks.
  - Files: `open_agent_core/memory.py`, `docker-compose.yaml`,
    `.env.example`, tests.
  - Complexity: large.
  - Depends on: MEM-001.

- [ ] **MEM-003:** Make the `MemoryStore` backend pluggable for stores
  outside this repository, not just the two built-in backends.
  - Acceptance:
    - The factory resolves a fully-qualified class path from
      `MEMORY_STORE_CLASS` ahead of the built-in URL-scheme detection, so a
      deployer can supply their own `MemoryStore` implementation without
      modifying `open-agent-core`.
    - `docs/memory.md` documents the protocol contract and gives a minimal
      third-party backend example.
  - Files: `open_agent_core/memory.py`, `docs/memory.md`.
  - Complexity: medium.
  - Depends on: MEM-001.

- [ ] **MEM-004:** Wire declared Agent Spec `memory` config into both engine
  invokers.
  - Acceptance:
    - `LangGraphAgentInvoker` and `AdkAgentInvoker` read `AgentIR.memory`
      and, when present, load prior turns from the configured `MemoryStore`
      before invoking the model and append the new turn after a successful
      response.
    - Agents that omit `memory` behave exactly as today — no behavior
      change for the common case.
    - Memory read/append failures are logged and degrade to no-memory
      behavior rather than failing the request, matching the existing
      `ADK_FALLBACK_ON_ERROR` fail-open convention.
  - Files: both `runner.py` (agent invokers), tests.
  - Complexity: large.
  - Depends on: MEM-001, MEM-002.

- [ ] **MEM-005:** Add memory-backend availability to health/readiness
  reporting.
  - Acceptance:
    - `GET /health/ready` additionally awaits the configured `MemoryStore`'s
      `ping()`, mirroring the existing checkpoint-store check from
      **RUN-004**, and returns 503 with a reason when unreachable.
    - A protected `GET /health/memory` route reports backend type and last
      successful ping, mirroring the existing `/health/mcp` route from
      **SEC-006**.
  - Files: both `runner.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small/medium.
  - Depends on: MEM-001, MEM-004.

- [ ] **MEM-006:** Document the pluggable memory architecture and update
  environment reference tables.
  - Acceptance:
    - `docs/memory.md` explains the `MemoryStore` protocol, built-in
      backends, the third-party plugin mechanism, and the availability
      health checks.
    - `README.md`'s environment variable table documents
      `MEMORY_STORE_PATH`, `MEMORY_DATABASE_URL`, and `MEMORY_STORE_CLASS`.
    - `docs/architecture.md` diagram gains a `MemoryStore` node parallel to
      the existing checkpoint-store flow.
    - `CHANGELOG.md` records the change.
  - Files: `docs/memory.md`, `README.md`, `docs/architecture.md`,
    `CHANGELOG.md`.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: MEM-001 through MEM-005.

## P2 - Default model configuration

Today, an agent that omits `llm_config.model_id`/`model` silently falls back
to a model name hardcoded inline at the call site — `"gpt-4o-mini"` in
`LangGraphAgentInvoker.invoke()` (`open_agent_lang/runner.py`) and
`"gemini-2.0-flash"` in `AdkCompiler.compile()`
(`open_agent_adk/compiler.py`). Neither fallback is configurable or
documented. The tasks below replace both inline fallbacks with one shared,
env-var-configurable resolver in `open-agent-core`, so a deployer can set an
organization-wide default model without editing every Agent Spec document,
and so the same resolution automatically covers any agent or sub-agent
either engine compiles — including nested agents from the planned ADK
workflow compiler (**COMPILE-009**) — rather than only the one call site
each engine happens to have today.

Precedence (highest to lowest): the agent's own declared
`llm_config.model_id`/`model` \> an engine-specific default env var \> a
generic cross-engine default env var \> the existing hardcoded built-in
fallback (preserved so zero-config behavior does not change).

- [ ] **MODEL-001:** Add a shared default-model resolution helper in
  `open-agent-core`.
  - Acceptance:
    - New `open_agent_core/model_config.py` exposes
      `resolve_model(llm_config, *, engine_env_var, builtin_default) -> str`
      implementing the precedence order above as a pure function.
    - Empty-string and unset env vars are treated identically (fall through
      to the next precedence level), matching existing env-parsing
      conventions elsewhere in the codebase.
    - Unit tests cover every precedence branch independently and in
      combination.
  - Files: new `open_agent_core/model_config.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.

- [ ] **MODEL-002:** Wire the LangGraph agent invoker to the shared resolver.
  - Acceptance:
    - `LangGraphAgentInvoker.invoke()` calls
      `resolve_model(llm_config, engine_env_var="OPENAI_DEFAULT_MODEL",
      builtin_default="gpt-4o-mini")` instead of its inline
      `.get(..., "gpt-4o-mini")` fallback chain.
    - Behavior is unchanged for existing agents when `OPENAI_DEFAULT_MODEL`
      and `DEFAULT_MODEL` are both unset.
  - Files: `open_agent_lang/runner.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: MODEL-001.

- [ ] **MODEL-003:** Wire the ADK compiler to the shared resolver.
  - Acceptance:
    - `AdkCompiler.compile()` calls
      `resolve_model(llm_config, engine_env_var="GOOGLE_DEFAULT_MODEL",
      builtin_default="gemini-2.0-flash")` instead of its inline
      `.get(..., "gemini-2.0-flash")` fallback chain.
    - Behavior is unchanged for existing agents when `GOOGLE_DEFAULT_MODEL`
      and `DEFAULT_MODEL` are both unset.
  - Files: `open_agent_adk/compiler.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: MODEL-001.

- [ ] **MODEL-004:** Apply the same default-model resolution to every
  nested/sub-agent either engine compiles, not just each engine's one
  current top-level call site.
  - Acceptance:
    - The planned ADK native workflow compiler (**COMPILE-009**) calls
      `resolve_model(...)` for every `LlmAgent` it builds inside a
      `Sequential`/`Parallel`/`LoopAgent` tree, using the same
      `GOOGLE_DEFAULT_MODEL` env var as the top-level agent path.
    - Any future LangGraph per-agent sub-graph compilation does the
      equivalent with `OPENAI_DEFAULT_MODEL`.
    - A regression test compiles a workflow with a `call` task referencing
      an agent that omits `model_id` and asserts the resolved default is
      used.
  - Files: `open_agent_adk/workflow_compiler.py` (once it exists per
    **COMPILE-009**), `open_agent_lang/compiler.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: MODEL-001, COMPILE-009.

- [ ] **MODEL-005:** Document default-model configuration and update
  environment reference tables.
  - Acceptance:
    - README's environment variable table documents `DEFAULT_MODEL`,
      `OPENAI_DEFAULT_MODEL`, and `GOOGLE_DEFAULT_MODEL`, including the
      precedence order and the existing built-in fallback values.
    - `.env.example` includes the new variables, empty by default (no
      behavior change from today).
    - `docs/architecture.md` documents that model resolution is centralized
      in `open_agent_core/model_config.py` and applies to every agent and
      sub-agent either engine compiles.
    - `CHANGELOG.md` records the change.
  - Files: `README.md`, `.env.example`, `docs/architecture.md`,
    `CHANGELOG.md`.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: MODEL-001 through MODEL-004.

## P2 - Externalized agent configuration

Agent Spec documents hardcode every agent characteristic today — model,
temperature, provider auth, system prompt, tool bindings — as literal
values. Retargeting an agent for a different environment (dev/staging/prod,
a different provider account, a different default persona) means editing
and redeploying the spec document itself. There is no placeholder syntax
that lets a spec *declare* a characteristic's value as "read from this
environment variable, falling back to this default if unset," the way
Spring Boot's `${property.name:defaultValue}` lets a properties/YAML file
defer a value to the environment. This is a different, complementary
concern to [`MODEL-001`..`MODEL-005`](#p2---default-model-configuration):
those tasks give the *platform* a fallback for a field an agent's author
left out entirely; the tasks below let an agent's *author* explicitly mark
any field as environment-overridable, with its own author-chosen default.

The workflow task language already owns the `${...}` syntax for its own
runtime expression evaluator (`open_agent_core/expressions.py`, evaluated
against workflow payload/context, e.g. `${ .priority == "high" }`). To avoid
colliding with that, placeholder resolution below is scoped to Agent Spec
and `bindings.yaml` documents only — never to Open Workflow task bodies,
which keep their existing expression semantics unchanged.

- [ ] **CONFIG-001:** Design the placeholder syntax, precedence rules, and
  document scope.
  - Acceptance:
    - A short design note fixes the exact grammar: `${VAR_NAME}` (required —
      resolution fails if unset) and `${VAR_NAME:default}` (optional —
      falls back to the literal default text if the variable is unset or
      empty).
    - The design explicitly states the syntax applies only to Agent Spec
      documents (`agent_spec/loader.py`) and `bindings.yaml`
      (`tool_bindings`, `agent_calls`, `aliases`), never to Open Workflow
      task bodies, so it cannot collide with the existing expression
      evaluator's own `${...}` syntax.
    - The design states precedence for a single field: the field's own
      resolved placeholder value (env var if set, else the spec's inline
      default) always wins; composing this with the separate
      `MODEL-001`..`MODEL-005` engine-default layer is deferred to
      **CONFIG-006**.
    - Reviewed against the existing `agent-with-tools.yaml` and
      `agent-vertex-config.yaml` conformance fixtures to confirm the syntax
      reads naturally in nested `llm_config`/`auth` blocks.
  - Files: design note only (no code).
  - Complexity: small.

- [ ] **CONFIG-002:** Implement the placeholder-resolution utility in
  `open-agent-core`.
  - Acceptance:
    - New `open_agent_core/config_resolution.py` exposes
      `resolve_env_placeholders(value, *, env=os.environ) -> Any`, a pure
      function that recursively walks dicts/lists and rewrites every string
      leaf matching `${VAR_NAME}`/`${VAR_NAME:default}`; non-matching
      strings and non-string leaves pass through unchanged.
    - A required placeholder (`${VAR_NAME}`, no `:default`) with no
      matching env var raises a typed error carrying the variable name and
      the dotted path to the offending field.
    - An optional placeholder (`${VAR_NAME:default}`) with an unset or
      empty-string env var resolves to the literal default text.
    - A literal `${...}` that does not match `[A-Z_][A-Z0-9_]*` (with or
      without `:default`) is left untouched, so it cannot misfire on
      unrelated bracketed text.
    - Pure unit tests cover: required-and-set, required-and-missing,
      optional-with-default-used, optional-with-env-override, nested
      dict/list structures, and adjacent non-placeholder text in the same
      string.
  - Files: new `open_agent_core/config_resolution.py`, new
    `tests/test_config_resolution.py`.
  - Complexity: small/medium.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-001.

- [ ] **CONFIG-003:** Wire placeholder resolution into the Agent Spec
  loader.
  - Acceptance:
    - `agent_spec/loader.py` calls `resolve_env_placeholders` on the raw
      parsed document dict before it reaches
      `AgentSpecDeserializer().from_dict(...)` and the `AgentDocument`
      pydantic model, so `llm_config.model_id`, `llm_config.temperature`,
      `llm_config.auth.*`, `system_prompt`, and `tools[].binding` can all
      carry placeholders.
    - A required-and-missing placeholder surfaces through the existing
      `SpecValidationError`/diagnostics path (see **CONFIG-005**), not a
      raw exception.
    - Existing agent fixtures with no placeholders load with byte-identical
      results (pure passthrough when no `${...}` pattern is present).
  - Files: `open_agent_core/agent_spec/loader.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-002.

- [ ] **CONFIG-004:** Wire placeholder resolution into `bindings.yaml`
  loading.
  - Acceptance:
    - `loader.py`'s `bindings.yaml` parsing resolves placeholders in
      `tool_bindings[].command`, `tool_bindings[].args`,
      `tool_bindings[].env` values, `agent_calls` targets, and `aliases`
      targets, so a deployment can point bindings at environment-specific
      commands/paths/targets without forking `bindings.yaml` per
      environment.
    - `BindingsIR` construction happens after resolution, so downstream
      code never sees an unresolved placeholder.
    - Existing bindings fixtures with no placeholders are unaffected.
  - Files: `open_agent_core/loader.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-002.

- [ ] **CONFIG-005:** Add a dedicated diagnostic code for unresolved
  required placeholders.
  - Acceptance:
    - A new diagnostic code (e.g. `AGENT_CONFIG_PLACEHOLDER_UNRESOLVED`) is
      added to `diagnostics.py`, raised by **CONFIG-003**/**CONFIG-004**
      through the same `SpecValidationError` path used by every other
      loader failure, including the variable name and source document
      location.
    - All three CLIs' existing diagnostic-rendering path (**CODE-002**)
      renders it without a raw traceback.
  - Files: `open_agent_core/diagnostics.py`, `agent_spec/loader.py`,
    `loader.py`, tests.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-003, CONFIG-004.

- [ ] **CONFIG-006:** Document and compose this mechanism with the
  engine-level default-model resolver.
  - Acceptance:
    - Documentation states the combined precedence when both mechanisms are
      in play: an agent's own literal or placeholder-resolved
      `llm_config.model_id` (this section) always wins; only when the
      field is omitted entirely does `resolve_model()` from
      [`MODEL-001`](#p2---default-model-configuration) apply.
    - No behavior change to `MODEL-001`..`MODEL-005`'s own resolver; this
      task is documentation/composition only, not a rewrite.
  - Files: `docs/architecture.md` or a new `docs/agent-config.md`.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-003, MODEL-001.

- [ ] **CONFIG-007:** Add conformance fixtures and end-to-end tests
  demonstrating placeholder usage.
  - Acceptance:
    - New fixtures under `tests/fixtures/conformance/agents/` (e.g.
      `agent-with-env-placeholders.yaml`) declare `llm_config.model_id`,
      `temperature`, and a tool `binding` using both `${VAR}` and
      `${VAR:default}` forms.
    - A `bindings.yaml` fixture variant declares a `tool_bindings` entry
      whose `command`/`env` use placeholders.
    - Parametrized tests load each fixture with the env var set and unset
      (asserting the default applies) and assert the resolved `AgentIR`
      values match expectations.
  - Files: `tests/fixtures/conformance/agents/**`, `tests/test_conformance.py`,
    tests.
  - Complexity: small/medium.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-003, CONFIG-004.

- [ ] **CONFIG-008:** Document the feature and update environment
  reference material.
  - Acceptance:
    - README gains a section (near "Agent capabilities") explaining the
      `${VAR}`/`${VAR:default}` syntax, its scope (Agent Spec and
      `bindings.yaml` only, distinct from the workflow expression
      language), and a worked example.
    - `.env.example` gains a comment block showing a sample placeholder
      usage pattern (no new required variables — placeholder names are
      author-chosen per spec, not fixed by the platform).
    - `docs/architecture.md` gains a short note on where resolution
      happens in the load pipeline (between raw YAML/JSON parse and
      Pydantic validation).
    - `CHANGELOG.md` records the change.
  - Files: `README.md`, `.env.example`, `docs/architecture.md`,
    `CHANGELOG.md`.
  - Complexity: small.
  - Depends on: CONFIG-001 through CONFIG-007.

